  exp: "Infantile Hypertrophic Pyloric Stenosis presents in the first 3-6 weeks of life with non-bilious projectile vomiting. The relentless vomiting of gastric hydrochloric acid (HCl) leads to loss of hydrogen and chloride ions, causing a Hypochloremic Metabolic Alkalosis. In response to hypovolemia, the kidneys activate the RAAS, retaining sodium at the expense of excreting potassium and paradoxically excreting hydrogen ions, leading to Hypokalemia and a paradoxical aciduria.",
  wrongExp: "• <strong>Option A (Hyperchloremic metabolic acidosis):</strong> Seen in severe diarrhea (loss of bicarbonate) or Renal Tubular Acidosis, not severe vomiting.<br>• <strong>Option C (Hyponatremic metabolic acidosis):</strong> Seen in Congenital Adrenal Hyperplasia (21-hydroxylase deficiency), which can also present with vomiting but is usually accompanied by virilization or shock.<br>• <strong>Option D (Hyperkalemic respiratory alkalosis):</strong> A mixed disorder not seen in upper GI losses."
