  exp: "Painless, massive lower gastrointestinal bleeding in a toddler is the classic presentation of a Meckel's diverticulum containing ectopic gastric mucosa. The ectopic gastric mucosa secretes acid, ulcerating the adjacent normal ileal mucosa and causing bleeding. The diagnostic test of choice is the Technetium-99m pertechnetate scan (Meckel's scan), because this radioisotope has a high affinity for taking up and highlighting gastric mucosa.",
  wrongExp: "• <strong>Option A (Abdominal ultrasound):</strong> The test of choice for intussusception or pyloric stenosis, but lacks sensitivity for a bleeding Meckel's diverticulum.<br>• <strong>Option C (Barium enema):</strong> Diagnostic and therapeutic for intussusception, but not useful for identifying ectopic gastric mucosa.<br>• <strong>Option D (Upper GI endoscopy):</strong> Meckel's diverticulum is located in the distal ileum (2 feet from the ileocecal valve), far beyond the reach of standard upper endoscopy."
